M4 the host grammar -- the negative corpus

PROVENANCE: OURS, DERIVED FROM THE ABNF (not extracted from the RFC text)

This directory is UNLIKE test/conformance/rfc3986/, whose every file is DERIVED
from the vendored RFC text by cmake/UriCorpus.cmake and re-derived by the lane
uri.harness.corpus-provenance. RFC 3986 lists no invalid URIs, so there is
nothing to extract: negative.scm is HAND-AUTHORED, derived from the collected
ABNF at test/conformance/rfc3986/rfc3986.abnf (§3.2, itself RFC-derived) and RFC
5234 Appendix B for ALPHA / DIGIT / HEXDIG. It is the M4 sibling of
test/conformance/charset/ (M3).

It is therefore a SEPARATE provenance with a SEPARATE count (decision 0005 item 3,
decision 0025 item 7): it is never averaged into the normative §5.4 / §1.1.2
figures, and it is NOT guarded by the corpus-provenance lane. Its guard is
instead:

  - every row's (component, code, offset) is cross-checked against
    (uri test host-oracle) -- an INDEPENDENT reading of the same ABNF -- by the
    lane uri.unit.host-oracle; a row inconsistent with the oracle reddens
    (decision 0025 item 7, oracle discipline of decisions 0005 / 0012);
  - per-production coverage floors keep the set from silently shrinking; and
  - in M4 Slices ①②, (uri host) is run over this corpus through uri-parse-validate
    and must reject every row with the recorded code and absolute offset, and over
    the positive corpora (§5.4, §1.1.2) which it must still ACCEPT.

WHAT IS HERE

  negative.scm   invalid authority inputs for userinfo / host (reg-name,
                 IPv4address, IP-literal, IPv6address) / port and dec-octet, each
                 tagged with the ABNF production it violates and the ABSOLUTE
                 offset of the offending position (the "%" for a malformed
                 pct-encoded; the opening "[" for any IP-literal-interior fault,
                 including a dec-octet range error -- decision 0025 items 3-4).
                 Every input is "http://<authority>...", so the authority begins
                 at offset 7. Every offset is defended in the row's note.

TWO M4-SPECIFIC INVARIANTS THE CORPUS PINS (decision 0025 item 3)

  - dec-octet is REACHABLE ONLY inside a bracketed literal. A bare "999.1.1.1"
    fails IPv4address but is a valid reg-name (§3.2.2 first-match-wins), so it is
    a valid host, not an error. Both invalid-dec-octet rows are inside "[" ... "]".
  - the split visits userinfo → host → port left to right. The last two rows are
    TWO-FAULT authorities whose earlier fault is the one reported, so a reordered
    validator reddens.

WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY

  scheme / path / query / fragment charsets are M3's (test/conformance/charset/).
  The abnfgen positive generator and a host-aware mutator (decision 0012) stay
  deferred v0.2 tooling; this corpus is hand-derived, as M3's was.
